{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790959981,"nonce":"/KiBFI6+ZOLI80SIn2k/HkGZVGQ=","title":"Ash's SSA interpreter runs the full AIR optimiser on every function before its first call","message":"Measured 2026-10-02 on the gpu plugin fixture (plugins/fixtures, `target/debug/gpu`, hybrid mode), Time Profiler, main thread, 1 ms samples: 73–96 of about 380 samples sit in `ash_interp::ssa::Cache::prepare` → `ash_core::air_pipeline::optimized_with_config` → `optimize_full`, mostly SROA's `plan_for` and GVN's `replace_all_uses`. The count is the same with Ash's crates at opt-level 1 or 3 in caribou's dev profile, so it is work done, not an unoptimised build.\n\nAt startup most functions run once. Optimising each one fully before interpreting it costs more than the interpretation saves. `interpreter_config_for` already prepares a function with no back edge more cheaply; the remaining cost is the functions that have loops but run them briefly.\n\nAsh's interpreter is Ash's design; this records what it costs a hosted program's startup. A cheaper first preparation (or optimising only once a function is warm, as the tiers already decide) would remove most of it.","files":null}]}